15. 3Sum

Given an integer array nums, return all the triplets [nums[i], nums[j], nums[k]] such that i != j, i != k, and j != k, and nums[i] + nums[j] + nums[k] == 0.
Notice that the solution set must not contain duplicate triplets.

Explanation:

First, create a set with List in it to store unique elements in it.
Next start fiterating using the first loop. 
Then before starting the second loop, make a hashset to note the elements of the second loop that we are going to iterate.
This is becuase int hte second loop, the pointer, ie; j, will change, so to keep track of j- that is if we have seen it before or not we need this.

In the second loop , calculate the third element -> -(arr[i]+arr[j]).

Next we will check with the hashSet if we have seen this element before cause this could be over third element.
if it exists, 
put all the 3 elements to a temporary list, sort it and add it to the Ans list (ar top one).
also at the end add the j element to hashset.

return th Set<List>> as an ArrayList

Code:

class Solution {
    // Function to find triplets with sum zero
    public List<List<Integer>> threeSum(int[] arr, int n) {
        // Store unique triplets
        Set<List<Integer>> ans = new HashSet<>();

        // First loop for first element
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            // Set to store elements seen in this iteration
            Set<Integer> hashset = new HashSet<>();

            // Second loop for second element
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                // Calculate third element needed
                int third = -(arr[i] + arr[j]);

                // If third already in set, we found a triplet
                if (hashset.contains(third)) {
                    List<Integer> temp = Arrays.asList(arr[i], arr[j], third);
                    Collections.sort(temp);
                    ans.add(temp);
                }

                // Add current element to set
                hashset.add(arr[j]);
            }
        }

        // Convert set to list
        return new ArrayList<>(ans);
    }
}

 
